WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 77: The Great Louisville Flood of 1937: High Water and the Breadline

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.840
[Rebecca] Welcome back to most fascinating stories.

2
00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:07.120
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:16.640
[Rebecca] In January 1937, in the depths of the Great Depression, the Ohio River unleashed the most devastating natural disaster in Midwestern history.

4
00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:28.800
[Rebecca] Over 30 days, 19 inches of freezing winter rain inundated the river basin, sending the Ohio cresting at an unfathomable 57 feet, nearly 30 feet above flood stage.

5
00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:40.160
[Rebecca] The muddy torrent swallowed 70% of Louisville, Kentucky, leaving 200,000 people homeless without drinking water, heat, or electricity in sub-freezing temperatures.

6
00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:48.960
[Rebecca] Amidst the floating wreckage and heroic boat rescues, photojournalist Margaret Burkwhite snapped one of the most famous images of the 20th century.

7
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:55.680
[Rebecca] George brings us the harrowing story of the 1937 Louisville flood.

8
00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:16.080
[George] Beginning in late December, a massive high-pressure ridge stalled over Bermuda, pumping an endless atmospheric river of warm, moisture-laden tropical air from the Gulf of Mexico directly into the Ohio River Valley.

9
00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:21.200
[George] There, it collided with a brutal Arctic polar front anchored over the Great Lakes.

10
00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:28.960
[George] For 30 consecutive days, relentless, torrential freezing downpours battered Kentucky, Indiana, and Ohio.

11
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:37.120
[George] Over 19 inches of rain fell on ground that was already saturated and frozen solid, unable to absorb a single droplet.

12
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:42.880
[George] Every tributary, creek, and mountain run poured its swollen volume into the Ohio River.

13
00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:49.520
[George] Old river pilots who had sailed the Mississippi Basin for 50 years watched the river gauges with mounting dread.

14
00:01:49.520 --> 00:02:04.1000
[George] By mid-January, the muddy river was rising at an astonishing rate of more than a foot every six hours, creeping over natural limestone bluffs, topping municipal flood walls, and expanding into an immense, churning brown inland sea miles wide.

15
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:20.040
[George] Meteorologists recorded that during the peak of the deluge, the Ohio River basin received over 165 trillion pounds of water, an atmospheric deluge equivalent to the volume of Lake Erie falling onto three states in 30 days.

16
00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:30.680
[George] Mountain runoff from the Allegheny and Cumberland Ranges cascaded into swollen creeks, tearing away wooden bridges and washing entire farmsteads into the churning brown torrent.

17
00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:43.080
[George] Hydrologists estimated that the total volume of rain falling across the Ohio Valley in January 1937 would have covered the entire state of Kentucky beneath 16 inches of solid water.

18
00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:54.280
[George] On Thursday, January 21, 1937, known forever in Kentucky history as Black Sunday, the river overwhelmed Louisville with apocalyptic speed.

19
00:02:54.280 --> 00:03:06.200
[George] The Ohio rose past its historic flood stage of 28 feet, surged past the previous 1884 record, and finally crested at an unbelievable 57.1 feet.

20
00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:13.080
[George] 70% of the city was submerged beneath 5 to 30 feet of ice cold, turbulent river water.

21
00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:24.920
[George] Downtown commercial streets were transformed into rushing canals where rowboats and motorized skiffs navigated past the second story windows of department stores, hotels, and bank vaults.

22
00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:33.480
[George] Over 230,000 residents, more than half the city's population, were forced from their homes in freezing January weather.

23
00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:47.040
[George] Families scrambled onto rooftops, climbed into attics with axes to chop their way through the roof if the water continued rising, and huddled shivering in cold winter winds, waiting for rescue boats to navigate the flooded avenues.

24
00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:58.720
[George] Downtown department stores like Stewart's and the Brown Hotel became eerie subterranean caverns where murky water lapped against crystal chandeliers and velvet display cases on the mezzanine levels.

25
00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:11.680
[George] Panic-stricken citizens paddled homemade wooden rafts through flooded avenues, shouting into the fog to locate missing relatives as floating pianos, front porches, and telephone poles drifted past in the current.

26
00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:23.360
[George] Rowboats tied up to second-story bedroom windows of Victorian homes, while family members passed crying infants and family heirlooms across the sill into the steady hands of volunteer boatmen.

27
00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:29.920
[George] The disaster rapidly intensified as critical municipal infrastructure collapsed beneath the floodwaters.

28
00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:44.480
[George] Shortly before midnight on Black Sunday, water flooded the basements of the Louisville Gas and Electric Company's Riverside Generating Station, extinguishing boiler fires and plunging the entire metropolis into total freezing darkness.

29
00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:54.080
[George] Hours later, the municipal water pumping station on River Road was inundated, contaminating the city's water mains with raw, muddy river silt.

30
00:04:54.080 --> 00:05:03.200
[George] With no electricity, no running drinking water, and no residential heat as temperatures plunged into the twenties, Louisville was paralyzed.

31
00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:14.480
[George] Telephones went dead, streetcar service ceased as electrical substations shorted out, and commercial radio station WHAS became the city's sole voice of survival.

32
00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:21.040
[George] Broadcasters worked around the clock by candlelight, transmitting thousands of emergency rescue bulletins.

33
00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:28.560
[George] Send a boat to 1244 Rowan Street, family of six on the roof shivering, or send milk and blankets to St.

34
00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.560
[George] Anthony Hospital, basement flooded.

35
00:05:31.560 --> 00:05:45.240
[George] WHAS radio engineers constructed makeshift antenna arrays on high ground, operating on emergency battery power to maintain continuous broadcast contact with federal disaster coordinators in Washington.

36
00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:57.720
[George] Announcers worked until their voices broke, reading over 100,000 individual emergency telegrams and coordinating water rescues that saved thousands of hypothermic citizens from rooftop perches.

37
00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:10.440
[George] Local newspapers improvised emergency editions printed on hand presses in elevated suburbs, distributing warnings about contaminated drinking water and instructions for boiling every drop before consumption.

38
00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:17.480
[George] In response to the catastrophe, an extraordinary grassroots rescue armada mobilized across the Upper South.

39
00:06:17.480 --> 00:06:36.040
[George] The United States Coast Guard dispatched hundreds of surfboats and cutters by rail from coastal stations in Massachusetts and Virginia, while fishermen, duck hunters, and boatbuilders from as far away as Michigan and Tennessee hitched wooden rowboats to their automobiles and drove straight to the floodwaters.

40
00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:49.800
[George] Over 3,000 boats navigated Louisville's flooded street corridors day and night, powered by oars and small outboard motors, dodging submerged streetcars, floating telephone poles, and live electrical wires.

41
00:06:49.800 --> 00:07:09.560
[George] Brave civilian volunteers and Coast Guard surfmen pulled thousands of hypothermic women, children, and elderly citizens from frozen roofs and second-story porches, transporting them to high ground along Eastern Parkway and Cherokee Park, which had become islands of refuge in a vast muddy archipelago.

42
00:07:09.560 --> 00:07:24.640
[George] Coast Guard surfmen who had battled Atlantic hurricanes marveled at the bizarre perils of navigating submerged city streets, where hidden underwater obstacles like iron fences, clotheslines and street signs threatened to capsize wooden surfboats.

43
00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:36.240
[George] Volunteers rode through blizzard winds for 18 hours without rest, wrapping hypothermic children in donated blankets and ferrying them to dry railroad depots on the southern bluffs.

44
00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:47.760
[George] Coast Guard commanders established headquarters at the Brown Hotel, organizing scheduled relief flotillas that patrolled submerged neighborhoods every 20 minutes to evacuate stranded residents.

45
00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:54.880
[George] As if drowning in icy water were not enough, the city faced the terrifying paradox of fire amidst the flood.

46
00:07:54.880 --> 00:08:12.560
[George] In the industrial west end, massive storage tanks at oil refineries and bulk petroleum distributors were lifted from their concrete foundations by the buoyant force of the rising river, rupting pipes and spilling hundreds of thousands of gallons of gasoline and kerosene onto the water's surface.

47
00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:19.520
[George] On January 26, a stray spark ignited the floating fuel slick near 12th and main streets.

48
00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:26.400
[George] A terrifying wall of flame erupted from the surface of the flood, burning out of control across several city blocks.

49
00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:34.000
[George] Firefighters were powerless, their horse-drawn and motorized steam pumpers were submerged, and the streets were canals.

50
00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:50.640
[George] In an astonishing feat of emergency improvisation, firefighters mounted portable chemical pumps onto flat bottomed wooden barges, paddling directly through the smoke to extinguish burning warehouse roofs before the floating fire could incinerate the stranded residential neighborhoods.

51
00:08:50.640 --> 00:09:03.720
[George] The blazing gasoline slick formed a floating river of fire that illuminated the storm clouds with an apocalyptic crimson glare, sending columns of black oily smoke billowing across the submerged west end of the city.

52
00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:16.280
[George] Firefighters risked their lives paddling flatboats within yards of the inferno, operating portable chemical extinguishers to prevent the floating flames from igniting lumberyards and grain elevators along the canal.

53
00:09:16.280 --> 00:09:27.720
[George] The chemical barges operated with heroic precision, spraying foam directly onto floating timber and gasoline slicks while blistering heat-peeled paint from the hulls of the rescue boats.

54
00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:36.840
[George] As floodwaters began their agonizingly slow retreat, life for the displaced working-class population was a grim struggle for basic survival.

55
00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:50.280
[George] In the hard-hit black neighborhood of the West End, hundreds of impoverished flood victims lined up in freezing rain to receive emergency rations of bread and hot soup from the American Red Cross Relief Center on 12th Street.

56
00:09:50.280 --> 00:10:02.760
[George] Passing through the city on assignment for Life magazine, legendary photojournalist Margaret Burkwhite stepped from a relief boat and noticed a visual juxtaposition that captured the soul of Depression-era America.

57
00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:22.040
[George] Directly behind the line of weary, freezing African American men, women, and children stood a giant national association of manufacturers' promotional billboard depicting an idealized white suburban family smiling in a convertible car, beneath the bold headline, World's highest standard of living.

58
00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:24.600
[George] There's no way like the American way.

59
00:10:24.600 --> 00:10:35.320
[George] Burkwhite's photograph became an instant global masterpiece, exposing the bitter racial and economic ironies of a nation suffering under economic collapse and natural disaster.

60
00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:49.200
[George] Margaret Burkwhite's photograph for Life magazine became an enduring visual metaphor of the Great Depression, crystallizing the deep chasm between corporate propaganda and the harsh economic reality facing millions of working class families.

61
00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:59.520
[George] The photograph was reprinted across the globe, challenging Americans to confront systemic poverty and racial inequality in the midst of national natural disasters.

62
00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:12.000
[George] Burkwhite later described the haunting quiet of the breadline, where freezing men stood in dignified silence beneath the billboard, their eyes reflecting the quiet endurance of a generation tested by crisis.

63
00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:20.160
[George] By mid-February, the Ohio River finally receded back within its banks, revealing a nightmarish landscape of devastation.

64
00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:26.720
[George] Over four feet of putrid river mud, dead livestock, and soaked plaster-coated homes and storefronts.

65
00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:36.800
[George] The property damage exceeded $250 million, equivalent to billions today, while nearly 400 lives were lost along the Ohio River basin.

66
00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:40.560
[George] Yet the people of Louisville met the ruin with astonishing grit.

67
00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:54.080
[George] Citizens formed cleanup brigades, shoveling mud from church pews and schoolrooms, while public health nurses administered typhoid vaccinations to tens of thousands of residents, miraculously preventing a major epidemic.

68
00:11:54.080 --> 00:12:07.520
[George] Determined that the city would never drown again, civic leaders lobbied Congress to construct a monumental, multi-million dollar concrete floodwall and earthen levy system stretching 29 miles along the Louisville Riverfront.

69
00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:10.400
[George] Completed in 1956 by the U.S.

70
00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:23.440
[George] Army Corps of Engineers, the Great Wall of Louisville has successfully repelled every major Ohio River crest for 70 years, an enduring concrete shield born from the muddy waters of 1937.

71
00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:36.680
[George] The reconstruction of Louisville was a monumental triumph of civic solidarity, as labor unions, merchant associations, and church parishes worked side by side to scrub tons of river mud from historic neighborhoods.

72
00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:50.520
[George] Today, the monumental concrete flood wall stands as a silent sentinel along River Road, adorned with colorful murals that celebrate the heroic boatmen, nurses, and citizens who conquered the deluge of 1937.

73
00:12:50.520 --> 00:13:04.520
[George] The giant flood wall transformed Louisville Riverfront into a secure bastion against the Ohio River, allowing modern industrial parks and green riverfront parks to flourish safely behind its massive concrete ramparts.

74
00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:13.800
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

75
00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:21.480
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we race across Civil War, Georgia in the thrilling great locomotive chase of 1862.

